<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">The Use of Large Language Models (LLMs)</h2>

<div id="Ax1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We used a large language model (GPT) solely for minor writing assistance, such as grammar checking, language polishing, and improving readability. No content generation, ideation, experimental design, or analysis was performed by the LLM. All research contributions, technical content, and results presented in this paper are entirely the work of the authors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Proof of Theorem <span id="A1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Universal Winning Ticket</span></h2>

<div id="A1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof"><span id="A1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 2.4 (Universal Winning Ticket). ‣ 2 Winner Slices in Pretrained Networks ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a></span>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\ell(f_{\theta}(x_{i}),y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\ell(f_{\theta}(x_{i}),y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the empirical downstream loss on dataset <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\{(x_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\{(x_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, with pretrained initialization <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any slice <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\subseteq W^{(\ell)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\subseteq W^{(\ell)}</annotation></semantics></math>, define the restricted gradient</span></p>
<table id="A1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{M}\;=\;\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\tfrac{\partial\mathcal{L}(\theta)}{\partial(M\odot W^{(\ell)})}=\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}J_{M}(x_{i})^{\top}\nabla_{f}\ell(f_{\theta}(x_{i}),y_{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{M}\;=\;\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\tfrac{\partial\mathcal{L}(\theta)}{\partial(M\odot W^{(\ell)})}=\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}J_{M}(x_{i})^{\top}\nabla_{f}\ell(f_{\theta}(x_{i}),y_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="A1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{M}(x)=\tfrac{\partial f_{\theta}(x)}{\partial(M\odot W^{(\ell)})}\in\mathbb{R}^{k\times|M|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{M}(x)=\tfrac{\partial f_{\theta}(x)}{\partial(M\odot W^{(\ell)})}\in\mathbb{R}^{k\times|M|}</annotation></semantics></math> is the slice Jacobian and <math id="A1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{f}\ell(\cdot,y_{i})\in\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>f</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{f}\ell(\cdot,y_{i})\in\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the gradient with respect to logits. By assumption there exists at least one <math id="A1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{f}\ell(f_{\theta_{0}}(x_{i}),y_{i})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>f</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{f}\ell(f_{\theta_{0}}(x_{i}),y_{i})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and by the projection condition each <math id="A1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{M}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{M}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> has nontrivial overlap with the <math id="A1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\mathrm{task}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\mathrm{task}}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional task subspace. Hence <math id="A1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{M}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{M}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for all nonempty <math id="A1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume slice-restricted Lipschitz smoothness: there exists <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{M}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{M}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="A1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0}+M\odot\Delta)\leq\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})+g_{M}^{\top}\mathrm{vec}(\Delta)+\tfrac{L_{M}}{2}\|\mathrm{vec}(\Delta)\|_{2}^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>vec</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>vec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0}+M\odot\Delta)\leq\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})+g_{M}^{\top}\mathrm{vec}(\Delta)+\tfrac{L_{M}}{2}\|\mathrm{vec}(\Delta)\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">for all <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Delta\|_{2}\leq\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Delta\|_{2}\leq\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. Choosing <math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=-\alpha g_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=-\alpha g_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> yields</span></p>
<table id="A1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0}-\alpha M\odot g_{M})\leq\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})-\alpha\|g_{M}\|_{2}^{2}+\tfrac{L_{M}}{2}\alpha^{2}\|g_{M}\|_{2}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0}-\alpha M\odot g_{M})\leq\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})-\alpha\|g_{M}\|_{2}^{2}+\tfrac{L_{M}}{2}\alpha^{2}\|g_{M}\|_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">which is minimized at <math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\star}=1/L_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\star}=1/L_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus for any <math id="A1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,2/L_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,2/L_{M})</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain strict decrease</span></p>
<table id="A1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0}-\alpha M\odot g_{M})\leq\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})-\tfrac{\|g_{M}\|_{2}^{2}}{2L_{M}}&lt;\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0}-\alpha M\odot g_{M})\leq\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})-\tfrac{\|g_{M}\|_{2}^{2}}{2L_{M}}&lt;\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore every slice admits a loss-decreasing update.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next, let <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{M_{1},\dots,M_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{M_{1},\dots,M_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be slices chosen such that their Jacobians span the task subspace:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\mathrm{span}\{J_{M_{1}}(x),\dots,J_{M_{m}}(x)\}=k_{\mathrm{task}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>span</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\mathrm{span}\{J_{M_{1}}(x),\dots,J_{M_{m}}(x)\}=k_{\mathrm{task}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">By the spectral balance assumption such a finite <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq k_{\mathrm{task}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq k_{\mathrm{task}}</annotation></semantics></math> exists. Define the combined Jacobian</span></p>
<table id="A1.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\mathrm{combined}}(x)=[J_{M_{1}}(x)\;|\;\cdots\;|\;J_{M_{m}}(x)]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>combined</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mo>⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\mathrm{combined}}(x)=[J_{M_{1}}(x)\;|\;\cdots\;|\;J_{M_{m}}(x)].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">In the linearized (NTK) regime,</span></p>
<table id="A1.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta_{0}+\Delta\theta}(x)\approx f_{\theta_{0}}(x)+J(x)\Delta\theta," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta_{0}+\Delta\theta}(x)\approx f_{\theta_{0}}(x)+J(x)\Delta\theta,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">and restriction to updates supported on <math id="A1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{M_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{M_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> suffices to realize any perturbation in the <math id="A1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\mathrm{task}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\mathrm{task}}</annotation></semantics></math> subspace. Since losses such as cross-entropy are convex in the logits, it follows that for any <math id="A1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> there exist slice updates <math id="A1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{U_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{U_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="A1.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\!\Big(\theta_{0}+\sum_{i=1}^{m}M_{i}\odot U_{i}\Big)\leq\epsilon." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.520em;" width="0.520em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\!\Big(\theta_{0}+\sum_{i=1}^{m}M_{i}\odot U_{i}\Big)\leq\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p3.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.5.1" class="ltx_text">Hence the union of finitely many slices constitutes a global winning ticket.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A1.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.08513v1/ntk_1.png" id="A1.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/119;" width="548" height="119" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Layer 1</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A1.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.08513v1/ntk_5.png" id="A1.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/119;" width="548" height="119" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Layer 5</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A1.F5.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.08513v1/ntk_11.png" id="A1.F5.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/119;" width="548" height="119" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>Layer 11</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="A1.F5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PCA/NTK agreement across layers.</span>
Each row shows (i) the centered representation kernel <math id="A1.F5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\tilde{H}\tilde{H}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\tilde{H}\tilde{H}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>,
(ii) the reconstruction <math id="A1.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PP^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PP^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.F5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\tilde{H}V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\tilde{H}V</annotation></semantics></math>, (iii) the absolute
difference <math id="A1.F5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert K-PP^{\top}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert K-PP^{\top}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math>, and (iv) cumulative explained variance
from PCA on <math id="A1.F5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> versus eigenvalues from <math id="A1.F5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. Results are shown for
<span id="A1.F5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RoBERTa-base</span> layers 1, 5, and 11. Spectra and CEV curves overlap,
confirming Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 2.5 (PCA Decomposition of the Representation/Linearized NTK Kernel). ‣ 2 Winner Slices in Pretrained Networks ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> across depth.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>PCA Decomposition of the Representation/Linearized NTK Kernel)</h2>

<div id="A2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof"><span id="A2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 2.5 (PCA Decomposition of the Representation/Linearized NTK Kernel). ‣ 2 Winner Slices in Pretrained Networks ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a></span>.</h6>
<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{H}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{H}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> for the centered feature matrix. Since centering removes the mean in each feature coordinate, we have <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{rank}(\tilde{H})=:r\leq\min\{n-1,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{rank}(\tilde{H})=:r\leq\min\{n-1,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the thin singular value decomposition (SVD)</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{H}\;=\;USV^{\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{H}\;=\;USV^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> have orthonormal columns (<math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\top}U=V^{\top}V=I_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\top}U=V^{\top}V=I_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>), and <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\mathrm{diag}(s_{1},\dots,s_{r})\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\mathrm{diag}(s_{1},\dots,s_{r})\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> with singular values <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}\geq\cdots\geq s_{r}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}\geq\cdots\geq s_{r}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The empirical covariance of the centered features is</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\;=\;\frac{1}{n-1}\tilde{H}^{\top}\tilde{H}\;=\;\frac{1}{n-1}\,VS^{2}V^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\;=\;\frac{1}{n-1}\tilde{H}^{\top}\tilde{H}\;=\;\frac{1}{n-1}\,VS^{2}V^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> diagonalizes <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and its positive eigenvalues are</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda_{i}\;=\;\frac{s_{i}^{2}}{n-1},\qquad i=1,\dots,r," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}\;=\;\frac{s_{i}^{2}}{n-1},\qquad i=1,\dots,r,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">so that <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda=\mathrm{diag}(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{r})=\frac{1}{n-1}S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda=\mathrm{diag}(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{r})=\frac{1}{n-1}S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> gives the eigendecomposition <math id="A2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=V\Lambda V^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=V\Lambda V^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> stated in the lemma.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Define <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:=\tilde{H}V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:=\tilde{H}V</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the SVD, <math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=(USV^{\top})V=US" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(USV^{\top})V=US</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="A2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> has orthogonal columns and <math id="A2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PP^{\top}=(US)(US)^{\top}=US^{2}U^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PP^{\top}=(US)(US)^{\top}=US^{2}U^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, the centered feature Gram matrix (a.k.a. linearized representation kernel)</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\;=\;\tilde{H}\tilde{H}^{\top}\;=\;(USV^{\top})(VSU^{\top})\;=\;US^{2}U^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\;=\;\tilde{H}\tilde{H}^{\top}\;=\;(USV^{\top})(VSU^{\top})\;=\;US^{2}U^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore <math id="A2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=PP^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=PP^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> exactly, which is the desired decomposition.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The eigen-decomposition of <math id="A2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> follows immediately: because <math id="A2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=US^{2}U^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=US^{2}U^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> orthonormal, the nonzero eigenvalues of <math id="A2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> are the diagonal entries of <math id="A2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, namely <math id="A2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,r</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the relation <math id="A2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}^{2}=(n-1)\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}^{2}=(n-1)\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> derived above, the nonzero spectrum of <math id="A2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}:=(n-1)\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}:=(n-1)\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,r</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently, <math id="A2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> share the same nonzero eigenvectors in their respective spaces (<math id="A2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>) and have spectra that differ by the scalar factor <math id="A2.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. This scaling also implies equality of explained-variance ratios. Indeed,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{k}\lambda_{i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{r}\lambda_{j}}\;=\;\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{k}\tfrac{1}{n-1}s_{i}^{2}}{\sum_{j=1}^{r}\tfrac{1}{n-1}s_{j}^{2}}\;=\;\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{k}s_{i}^{2}}{\sum_{j=1}^{r}s_{j}^{2}}\;=\;\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{k}\mu_{i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{r}\mu_{j}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{k}\lambda_{i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{r}\lambda_{j}}\;=\;\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{k}\tfrac{1}{n-1}s_{i}^{2}}{\sum_{j=1}^{r}\tfrac{1}{n-1}s_{j}^{2}}\;=\;\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{k}s_{i}^{2}}{\sum_{j=1}^{r}s_{j}^{2}}\;=\;\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{k}\mu_{i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{r}\mu_{j}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">since <math id="A2.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}=s_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}=s_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence PCA explained-variance ratios computed from the feature covariance <math id="A2.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> coincide exactly with those computed from the Gram kernel <math id="A2.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">For completeness, one can also see the spectral correspondence without SVD, using the algebraic fact that <math id="A2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AB</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BA</annotation></semantics></math> have the same nonzero eigenvalues (including multiplicities). Taking <math id="A2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\tilde{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\tilde{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\tfrac{1}{n-1}\tilde{H}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\tfrac{1}{n-1}\tilde{H}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, the products <math id="A2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AB=\tfrac{1}{n-1}\tilde{H}\tilde{H}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AB=\tfrac{1}{n-1}\tilde{H}\tilde{H}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BA=\tfrac{1}{n-1}\tilde{H}^{\top}\tilde{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BA=\tfrac{1}{n-1}\tilde{H}^{\top}\tilde{H}</annotation></semantics></math> share their nonzero spectra; this gives again that the positive eigenvalues of <math id="A2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> equal <math id="A2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math> times those of <math id="A2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and yields the same variance-ratio identity.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, in the context of linearized training around <math id="A2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, when the readout is linear in the representation <math id="A2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{H}</annotation></semantics></math> or the network is considered in the (first-order) tangent regime, the kernel governing function-space updates reduces to the representation kernel <math id="A2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\tilde{H}\tilde{H}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\tilde{H}\tilde{H}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>; the lemma thus characterizes its spectrum through the PCA of <math id="A2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, completing the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Empirically, Figure <a href="#A1.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem Universal Winning Ticket ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> validates the PCA/NTK correspondence on <span id="A2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RoBERTa-base</span> using <math id="A2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}8</annotation></semantics></math> examples at layers 1, 5, and 11. For each layer we compute the centered representation kernel <math id="A2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\tilde{H}\tilde{H}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\tilde{H}\tilde{H}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> (“True NTK” panels), the PCA basis <math id="A2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="A2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=\frac{1}{n-1}\tilde{H}^{\top}\tilde{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\frac{1}{n-1}\tilde{H}^{\top}\tilde{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the reconstruction <math id="A2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PP^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PP^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\tilde{H}V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\tilde{H}V</annotation></semantics></math> (middle-left panels). The absolute difference maps <math id="A2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert K-PP^{\top}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert K-PP^{\top}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> (middle-right) are uniformly small, confirming the exact decomposition <math id="A2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=PP^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=PP^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> up to numerical error. The rightmost plots compare cumulative explained variance derived from the eigenvalues of <math id="A2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (“True PCA”) versus those induced by <math id="A2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (“PCA from NTK”); the curves overlap across depth, showing that PCA variance ratios in feature space match those of the representation kernel. Together, these results support Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 2.5 (PCA Decomposition of the Representation/Linearized NTK Kernel). ‣ 2 Winner Slices in Pretrained Networks ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> empirically across multiple layers.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark B.1</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span>(Relation to the NTK)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The identity <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\tilde{H}\tilde{H}^{\top}=PP^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\tilde{H}\tilde{H}^{\top}=PP^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> describes the (centered) representation kernel.
It coincides with the empirical NTK <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\theta_{0}}(x,x^{\prime})=\langle\nabla_{\theta}f_{\theta_{0}}(x),\nabla_{\theta}f_{\theta_{0}}(x^{\prime})\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\theta_{0}}(x,x^{\prime})=\langle\nabla_{\theta}f_{\theta_{0}}(x),\nabla_{\theta}f_{\theta_{0}}(x^{\prime})\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>
when the parameter subset with respect to which the NTK is computed corresponds to a linear readout over fixed features (e.g., last-layer weights with upstream layers frozen).
For general multi-layer adaptations, the full NTK requires Jacobians w.r.t. all trainable parameters and does not reduce to a pure feature Gram matrix.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Rank, NTK, and PCA</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section quantifies how <em id="A3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">domain familiarity</em> affects the slice rank needed for good adaptation. The main idea is simple: when the frozen backbone already organizes features along task-relevant directions, a small slice rank is enough; when the task is unfamiliar, a larger rank is needed. We make this precise using PCA on hidden features and the linearized NTK view: a steep PCA spectrum (high cumulative explained variance, CEV) indicates that the task subspace is low-dimensional and small slices should suffice; a flat spectrum indicates the opposite.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At the beginning, we warm-start <span id="A3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RoBERTa-base</span> on MRPC for 100 steps (batch size 64, BF16) to bias its features toward paraphrase semantics without fully adapting the model. We then fine-tune with SliceFine at ranks <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\{1,3,5,7\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\{1,3,5,7\}</annotation></semantics></math> on six GLUE tasks, and report three diagnostics that map directly to the analysis.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>PCA cumulative explained variance (CEV) Analysis</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For layer <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, let the centered features be <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{H}_{\ell}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d_{\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{H}_{\ell}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d_{\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the covariance <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\ell}=\frac{1}{n-1}\tilde{H}_{\ell}^{\top}\tilde{H}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\ell}=\frac{1}{n-1}\tilde{H}_{\ell}^{\top}\tilde{H}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, and eigenvalues <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}\geq\cdots\geq\lambda_{r_{\ell}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}\geq\cdots\geq\lambda_{r_{\ell}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. The CEV after <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> components is</p>
<table id="A3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CEV}_{\ell}(k)=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{k}\lambda_{i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{r_{\ell}}\lambda_{j}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>CEV</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msubsup><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CEV}_{\ell}(k)=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{k}\lambda_{i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{r_{\ell}}\lambda_{j}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which summarizes how concentrated the feature spectrum is.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.08513v1/cev.png" id="A3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/137;" width="548" height="137" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>PCA cumulative explained variance (CEV) for eight GLUE tasks. Tasks such as MRPC and QQP reach over 85% variance with few components, indicating a compact task subspace after MRPC warm-start. Tasks such as SST-2 and CoLA accumulate variance more slowly, suggesting higher intrinsic dimensionality and a need for larger slice ranks.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Interpretation of Figure <a href="#A3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ C.1 PCA cumulative explained variance (CEV) Analysis ‣ Appendix C Rank, NTK, and PCA ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. MRPC and QQP cross 85% variance with only a few principal components, consistent with a low-dimensional task subspace when the backbone is biased toward paraphrase semantics (As warm-start with MRPC dataset). In contrast, SST-2 and CoLA require more components, pointing to higher intrinsic dimensionality. This matches the rank rule-of-thumb: a steep spectrum suggests that a small slice rank is enough; a flatter spectrum suggests that a larger rank is needed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Prediction shift via Kullback–Leibler divergence</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> be the warm-started backbone and <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> the model after SliceFine with rank <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. For a <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-class problem with logits <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\theta}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\theta}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and probabilities <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(y\mid x)=\mathrm{softmax}(z_{\theta}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(y\mid x)=\mathrm{softmax}(z_{\theta}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>, define the dataset-averaged divergence</p>
<table id="A3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{KL}_{\mathcal{D}}(\theta_{r}\,\|\,\theta_{b})=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{c=1}^{k}p_{\theta_{r}}(c\mid x_{i})\,\log\!\frac{p_{\theta_{r}}(c\mid x_{i})}{p_{\theta_{b}}(c\mid x_{i})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>KL</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{KL}_{\mathcal{D}}(\theta_{r}\,\|\,\theta_{b})=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{c=1}^{k}p_{\theta_{r}}(c\mid x_{i})\,\log\!\frac{p_{\theta_{r}}(c\mid x_{i})}{p_{\theta_{b}}(c\mid x_{i})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Small KL means the fine-tuned model stays close to the backbone’s predictions (a “lazy” update); large KL means stronger shifts.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.08513v1/rank_slice_kl.png" id="A3.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/170;" width="548" height="170" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Accuracy (bars), base accuracy (hatched bars), and KL divergence to the warm-started backbone (lines) across six GLUE tasks (MRPC, QQP, MNLI, QNLI, SST-2, CoLA) for different slice ranks. Tasks close to MRPC (MRPC, QQP) saturate at very low rank and show small KL; tasks with flatter CEV (SST-2, CoLA) benefit from higher ranks and show larger KL.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Interpretation of Figure <a href="#A3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ C.2 Prediction shift via Kullback–Leibler divergence ‣ Appendix C Rank, NTK, and PCA ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. MRPC and QQP achieve high accuracy with <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math> and display low KL, consistent with high task energy already present in the backbone. SST-2 and CoLA gain from larger ranks and show higher KL, indicating that more task-relevant directions must be engaged to move predictions away from the warm-start.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Layer-wise representation change via centered kernel alignment (CKA)</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For layer <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{b}^{(\ell)},H_{r}^{(\ell)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d_{\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{b}^{(\ell)},H_{r}^{(\ell)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d_{\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math> be hidden states from <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>; center by <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{H}:=H-\frac{1}{n}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^{\top}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>𝟏𝟏</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{H}:=H-\frac{1}{n}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^{\top}H</annotation></semantics></math>. Linear CKA is</p>
<table id="A3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CKA}\!\left(H_{b}^{(\ell)},H_{r}^{(\ell)}\right)=\frac{\left\|\tilde{H}_{b}^{(\ell)\top}\tilde{H}_{r}^{(\ell)}\right\|_{F}^{2}}{\left\|\tilde{H}_{b}^{(\ell)\top}\tilde{H}_{b}^{(\ell)}\right\|_{F}\,\left\|\tilde{H}_{r}^{(\ell)\top}\tilde{H}_{r}^{(\ell)}\right\|_{F}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.287em;" width="2.287em"><mi>CKA</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CKA}\!\left(H_{b}^{(\ell)},H_{r}^{(\ell)}\right)=\frac{\left\|\tilde{H}_{b}^{(\ell)\top}\tilde{H}_{r}^{(\ell)}\right\|_{F}^{2}}{\left\|\tilde{H}_{b}^{(\ell)\top}\tilde{H}_{b}^{(\ell)}\right\|_{F}\,\left\|\tilde{H}_{r}^{(\ell)\top}\tilde{H}_{r}^{(\ell)}\right\|_{F}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Higher CKA means the representations stay closer to the backbone.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.08513v1/CKA.png" id="A3.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/140;" width="548" height="140" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Layer-wise CKA between pre- and post-fine-tuning representations for ranks 1, 3, 5, and 7 across MRPC, QQP, MNLI, QNLI, SST-2, and CoLA. Larger CKA indicates smaller feature drift. MRPC/QQP remain close to the backbone even at higher ranks; SST-2/CoLA show larger drift in upper layers as rank increases.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Interpretation of Figure <a href="#A3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ C.3 Layer-wise representation change via centered kernel alignment (CKA) ‣ Appendix C Rank, NTK, and PCA ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. For MRPC and QQP, CKA remains high in final layer even at larger ranks, consistent with a lazy regime: only modest adjustments are needed. For SST-2 and CoLA, CKA decreases as rank grows—especially in upper layers—showing that these tasks require broader subspace updates. CKA is a representation-level proxy; the kernel–PCA link that motivates this interpretation is given in Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 2.5 (PCA Decomposition of the Representation/Linearized NTK Kernel). ‣ 2 Winner Slices in Pretrained Networks ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, warming on MRPC increases CEV for semantically similar tasks, so low ranks work well and changes remain small (low KL, high CKA). For tasks with flatter CEV, larger ranks are needed, with larger prediction shifts and representation changes. These trends are exactly what the PCA/NTK view predicts: when the backbone concentrates task energy in a few directions, small slices suffice; when it does not, larger slices are required.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Backbone Dependence</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A slice can only reduce loss if its updates move the model along task-relevant directions already encoded by the pretrained backbone.
Formally, the loss gradient at the output is propagated back through the frozen features, and the effect of a slice update is controlled by two factors:
(i) the <em id="A4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">task energy</em> present in the backbone representations (how much of the feature variance lies in the subspace that separates the labels), and
(ii) the <em id="A4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">overlap</em> between the slice Jacobian and that task subspace.
If the pretrained network retains high principal energy on the task (large PCA cumulative explained variance), then all slices—by spectral balance—have nontrivial projection onto that subspace, yielding a nonzero restricted gradient and guaranteed decrease.
Conversely, if we ablate the backbone (e.g., heavy pruning or strong domain shift), the representation kernel loses energy, the accessible task dimension shrinks, and the slice gradient collapses unless we increase the slice rank.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma D.1</span></span><span id="A4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Backbone energy &amp; alignment condition for local winners)<span id="A4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be a pretrained network and, at layer <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, let
<math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\ell}=[\phi_{\ell}(x_{1}),\dots,\phi_{\ell}(x_{n})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\ell}=[\phi_{\ell}(x_{1}),\dots,\phi_{\ell}(x_{n})]</annotation></semantics></math> have singular values <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\sigma_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\sigma_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the top <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\mathrm{task}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\mathrm{task}}</annotation></semantics></math> left singular vectors and assume the task-energy ratio
<math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E:=\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{k_{\mathrm{task}}}\sigma_{j}^{2}}{\sum_{j}\sigma_{j}^{2}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msubsup></mstyle><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub></mstyle><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E:=\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{k_{\mathrm{task}}}\sigma_{j}^{2}}{\sum_{j}\sigma_{j}^{2}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a slice mask <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> in layer <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, define the feature-space slice Jacobian
<math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{M}^{\phi}(x)=\frac{\partial\phi_{\ell}(x)}{\partial(M\odot W^{(\ell)})}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\ell}\times|M|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{M}^{\phi}(x)=\frac{\partial\phi_{\ell}(x)}{\partial(M\odot W^{(\ell)})}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\ell}\times|M|}</annotation></semantics></math> and set</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\min}(M):=\sigma_{\min}\!\big(U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}^{\top}J_{M}^{\phi}(x)\big),\qquad\beta(M):=\big\|(I-P_{U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}})\,J_{M}^{\phi}(x)\big\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\min}(M):=\sigma_{\min}\!\big(U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}^{\top}J_{M}^{\phi}(x)\big),\qquad\beta(M):=\big\|(I-P_{U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}})\,J_{M}^{\phi}(x)\big\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <em id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">spectral balance</em>: there exist constants <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\gamma}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\gamma}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\min}(M)\geq\underline{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\min}(M)\geq\underline{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(M)\leq c\,\underline{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(M)\leq c\,\underline{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> for all admissible slices <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> be convex in the logits and let <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\mathrm{out}}^{(\ell)}=\frac{\partial f_{\theta_{0}}}{\partial\phi_{\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>out</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\mathrm{out}}^{(\ell)}=\frac{\partial f_{\theta_{0}}}{\partial\phi_{\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define the feature-space gradient <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}=(J_{\mathrm{out}}^{(\ell)})^{\top}\nabla_{f}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>out</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}=(J_{\mathrm{out}}^{(\ell)})^{\top}\nabla_{f}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and decompose
<math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}=g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}+g_{\phi,\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}=g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}+g_{\phi,\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}:=P_{U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}}g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}:=P_{U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}}g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi,\perp}:=(I-P_{U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}})g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⟂</mo></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi,\perp}:=(I-P_{U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}})g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume the <em id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">gradient alignment</em> condition: for some <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{\phi,\perp}\|\leq\rho\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|,\qquad\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⟂</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{\phi,\perp}\|\leq\rho\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|,\qquad\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume further that <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth along the slice coordinates (equivalently, <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\max}(H_{M})\leq L&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\max}(H_{M})\leq L&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{M}:=\nabla^{2}_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{M}:=\nabla^{2}_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>).
Then</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big\|\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})\big\|=\big\|(J_{M}^{\phi}(x))^{\top}g_{\phi}\big\|\;\geq\;(1-c\rho)\,\underline{\gamma}\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|\;&gt;\;0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn> 0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big\|\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})\big\|=\big\|(J_{M}^{\phi}(x))^{\top}g_{\phi}\big\|\;\geq\;(1-c\rho)\,\underline{\gamma}\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|\;&gt;\;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the slice-only update <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\star}=-\lambda_{\max}(H_{M})^{-1}\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\star}=-\lambda_{\max}(H_{M})^{-1}\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\big(\theta_{0}+M\odot U^{\star}\big)\;\leq\;\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})\;-\;\frac{(1-c\rho)^{2}\,\underline{\gamma}^{\,2}\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|^{2}}{2\,\lambda_{\max}(H_{M})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn> 2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\big(\theta_{0}+M\odot U^{\star}\big)\;\leq\;\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})\;-\;\frac{(1-c\rho)^{2}\,\underline{\gamma}^{\,2}\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|^{2}}{2\,\lambda_{\max}(H_{M})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We can Write <math id="A4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:=P_{U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}}=U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:=P_{U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}}=U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, an orthogonal projector
(<math id="A4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{2}=P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{2}=P</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\top}=P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\top}=P</annotation></semantics></math>), and recall that <math id="A4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A\|_{2}=\sigma_{\max}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A\|_{2}=\sigma_{\max}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\min}(A)=\inf_{\|z\|_{2}=1}\|Az\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\min}(A)=\inf_{\|z\|_{2}=1}\|Az\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The feature–space slice Jacobian is
<math id="A4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{M}^{\phi}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\ell}\times|M|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{M}^{\phi}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\ell}\times|M|}</annotation></semantics></math> and the feature gradient is
<math id="A4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}=(J_{\mathrm{out}}^{(\ell)})^{\top}\nabla_{f}\mathcal{L}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>out</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}=(J_{\mathrm{out}}^{(\ell)})^{\top}\nabla_{f}\mathcal{L}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the chain rule through <math id="A4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})=\Big(\tfrac{\partial\phi_{\ell}(x)}{\partial(M\odot W^{(\ell)})}\Big)^{\top}\tfrac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\phi_{\ell}(x)}=(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}g_{\phi}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})=\Big(\tfrac{\partial\phi_{\ell}(x)}{\partial(M\odot W^{(\ell)})}\Big)^{\top}\tfrac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\phi_{\ell}(x)}=(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}g_{\phi}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Decompose <math id="A4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> via the projector <math id="A4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> as
<math id="A4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}=g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}+g_{\phi,\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}=g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}+g_{\phi,\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="A4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}:=Pg_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}:=Pg_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi,\perp}:=(I-P)g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⟂</mo></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi,\perp}:=(I-P)g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which are orthogonal since <math id="A4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is orthogonal. Using equation <a href="#A4.E9" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix D Backbone Dependence ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and the triangle inequality,</span></p>
<table id="A4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})\|_{2}=\|(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}+(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}g_{\phi,\perp}\|_{2}\geq\|(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}-\|(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}g_{\phi,\perp}\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})\|_{2}=\|(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}+(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}g_{\phi,\perp}\|_{2}\geq\|(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}-\|(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}g_{\phi,\perp}\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the first term, note that <math id="A4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}=U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}^{\top}g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}=U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}^{\top}g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="A4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A:=(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}\in\mathbb{R}^{|M|\times k_{\mathrm{task}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A:=(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}\in\mathbb{R}^{|M|\times k_{\mathrm{task}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z:=U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}^{\top}g_{\phi}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\mathrm{task}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z:=U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}^{\top}g_{\phi}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\mathrm{task}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}=\|Az\|_{2}\geq\sigma_{\min}(A)\,\|z\|_{2}=\sigma_{\min}\!\big((J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}\big)\,\|U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}^{\top}g_{\phi}\|_{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}=\|Az\|_{2}\geq\sigma_{\min}(A)\,\|z\|_{2}=\sigma_{\min}\!\big((J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}\big)\,\|U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}^{\top}g_{\phi}\|_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the inequality uses the singular–value bound <math id="A4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|Az\|_{2}\geq\sigma_{\min}(A)\|z\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|Az\|_{2}\geq\sigma_{\min}(A)\|z\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because <math id="A4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}</annotation></semantics></math> has orthonormal columns, <math id="A4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}^{\top}g_{\phi}\|_{2}=\|Pg_{\phi}\|_{2}=\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}^{\top}g_{\phi}\|_{2}=\|Pg_{\phi}\|_{2}=\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By definition <math id="A4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\min}(M):=\sigma_{\min}(U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}^{\top}J_{M}^{\phi})=\sigma_{\min}((J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\min}(M):=\sigma_{\min}(U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}^{\top}J_{M}^{\phi})=\sigma_{\min}((J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence</span></p>
<table id="A4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}\geq\gamma_{\min}(M)\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}\geq\underline{\gamma}\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}\geq\gamma_{\min}(M)\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}\geq\underline{\gamma}\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">using the spectral–balance lower bound <math id="A4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\min}(M)\geq\underline{\gamma}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\min}(M)\geq\underline{\gamma}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the second term, use the operator–norm bound and the identity <math id="A4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A^{\top}\|_{2}=\|A\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A^{\top}\|_{2}=\|A\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}g_{\phi,\perp}\|_{2}=\|(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}(I-P)g_{\phi}\|_{2}\leq\|(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}(I-P)\|_{2}\,\|g_{\phi,\perp}\|_{2}=\|(I-P)J_{M}^{\phi}\|_{2}\,\|g_{\phi,\perp}\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⟂</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⟂</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}g_{\phi,\perp}\|_{2}=\|(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}(I-P)g_{\phi}\|_{2}\leq\|(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}(I-P)\|_{2}\,\|g_{\phi,\perp}\|_{2}=\|(I-P)J_{M}^{\phi}\|_{2}\,\|g_{\phi,\perp}\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">By definition <math id="A4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(M):=\|(I-P)J_{M}^{\phi}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(M):=\|(I-P)J_{M}^{\phi}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and by spectral balance <math id="A4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(M)\leq c\,\underline{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(M)\leq c\,\underline{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The alignment assumption gives <math id="A4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{\phi,\perp}\|_{2}\leq\rho\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⟂</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{\phi,\perp}\|_{2}\leq\rho\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore</span></p>
<table id="A4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}g_{\phi,\perp}\|_{2}\leq c\,\underline{\gamma}\,\rho\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|(J_{M}^{\phi})^{\top}g_{\phi,\perp}\|_{2}\leq c\,\underline{\gamma}\,\rho\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Combining equation <a href="#A4.E10" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix D Backbone Dependence ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, equation <a href="#A4.E11" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix D Backbone Dependence ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, and equation <a href="#A4.E12" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix D Backbone Dependence ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> yields</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})\|_{2}\geq\big(1-c\rho\big)\,\underline{\gamma}\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})\|_{2}\geq\big(1-c\rho\big)\,\underline{\gamma}\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\gamma}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\gamma}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> by assumption,
the right-hand side is strictly positive, establishing the claimed nonzero restricted gradient.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">To obtain a quantitative decrease, assume <math id="A4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth along the slice coordinates,
i.e. for any <math id="A4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in\mathbb{R}^{|M|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in\mathbb{R}^{|M|}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0}+M\odot U)\leq\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})+\langle\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0}),U\rangle+\tfrac{L}{2}\|U\|_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0}+M\odot U)\leq\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})+\langle\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0}),U\rangle+\tfrac{L}{2}\|U\|_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Choose the steepest–descent step <math id="A4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\star}:=-L^{-1}\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\star}:=-L^{-1}\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> (equivalently, <math id="A4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\lambda_{\max}(H_{M})^{-1}\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\lambda_{\max}(H_{M})^{-1}\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="A4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\lambda_{\max}(H_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\lambda_{\max}(H_{M})</annotation></semantics></math>).
Then</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0}+M\odot U^{\star})\leq\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})-\frac{1}{2L}\,\|\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})\|_{2}^{2}\leq\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})-\frac{(1-c\rho)^{2}\,\underline{\gamma}^{\,2}\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\,L}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn> 2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0}+M\odot U^{\star})\leq\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})-\frac{1}{2L}\,\|\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})\|_{2}^{2}\leq\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})-\frac{(1-c\rho)^{2}\,\underline{\gamma}^{\,2}\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\,L},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">where the last inequality substitutes the lower bound on <math id="A4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> derived above.
Taking <math id="A4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\lambda_{\max}(H_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\lambda_{\max}(H_{M})</annotation></semantics></math> gives the stated decrement, completing the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.08513v1/pruning.png" id="A4.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/141;" width="548" height="141" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>
Effect of pruning the frozen backbone on slice fine-tuning.
A fraction <math id="A4.F9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> of <em id="A4.F9.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-slice</em> pretrained weights is set to zero by global magnitude pruning
(bias and LayerNorm parameters are kept); the slice remains trainable and the rest of the backbone stays frozen.
Accuracy drops monotonically as <math id="A4.F9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> increases—mild for <math id="A4.F9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\!\leq\!0.2\text{--}0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">≤</mo><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>–</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\!\leq\!0.2\text{--}0.3</annotation></semantics></math>, steep for <math id="A4.F9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\!\geq\!0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">≥</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\!\geq\!0.6</annotation></semantics></math>, and worst at <math id="A4.F9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{=}1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{=}1.0</annotation></semantics></math> when the backbone is fully ablated.
This confirms that slices rely on the pretrained scaffold: pruning reduces representation energy (lower PCA CEV / NTK mass), shrinking the slice’s effective task overlap and requiring larger ranks to compensate.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To validate backbone dependence empirically, we design two controlled tests that isolate the role of the frozen backbone while keeping training schedules (epochs, batch size, sequence length, BF16) fixed.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Backbone pruning</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix D Backbone Dependence ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> progressively prunes a fraction <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> of non-slice weights to zero before fine-tuning (slice trainable; biases/LayerNorm kept).
Across SST-2, QNLI, STS-B, and MRPC, performance degrades smoothly with <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.
For modest pruning the drop is small, but heavy pruning causes large losses, and removing the backbone entirely (<math id="A4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>) yields the worst performance.
This pattern matches the PCA/NTK view: pruning alters the centered feature matrix <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, decreasing the principal energy of the representation kernel <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\tilde{H}\tilde{H}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\tilde{H}\tilde{H}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> and shrinking the accessible task subspace, so a fixed-rank slice captures less of what matters.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F10" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.08513v1/initilization.png" id="A4.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/147;" width="548" height="147" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>
Slice initialization sensitivity on <span id="A4.F10.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SST-2</span>, <span id="A4.F10.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">QNLI</span>, <span id="A4.F10.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">STS-B</span>, and <span id="A4.F10.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MRPC</span>.
For ranks <math id="A4.F10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\{1,5,10,20\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\{1,5,10,20\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compare <em id="A4.F10.12" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pretrained</em> slices (solid) to <em id="A4.F10.13" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">newly initialized</em> slices (dashed; zero/Xavier/Kaiming/LeCun), while the backbone remains frozen at pretrained values.
Curves converge to similar performance after a short warm-up, indicating that final accuracy is driven by the backbone’s features and the slice’s task overlap, not by the slice’s initial values.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Slice re-initialization</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A4.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ D.1 Backbone pruning ‣ Appendix D Backbone Dependence ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> compares pretrained vs. randomly reinitialized slices for ranks <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\{1,5,10,20\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\{1,5,10,20\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
After a brief warm-up, both settings reach nearly the same accuracy on all four tasks.
This behavior is consistent with the linearized view: with the backbone frozen, the slice acts through its Jacobian block, so optimization is locally quadratic in the slice parameters and converges to a similar solution regardless of the slice’s starting point (provided the backbone retains high task energy).
In contrast, reinitializing and freezing the <em id="A4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">backbone</em> (while training only the slice) severely hurts performance, reinforcing that slices succeed because they ride on a strong pretrained scaffold.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, we see,
(i) Strong backbones are essential: degrading them reduces PCA CEV / NTK mass and harms slice effectiveness.
(ii) Slice initialization is largely irrelevant; the backbone’s features determine the attainable accuracy.
(iii) These trends align with the bounds in Lemma <a href="#A4.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma D.1 (Backbone energy &amp; alignment condition for local winners). ‣ Appendix D Backbone Dependence ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> and the PCA/NTK equivalence (Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 2.5 (PCA Decomposition of the Representation/Linearized NTK Kernel). ‣ 2 Winner Slices in Pretrained Networks ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>): when backbone task energy is high, even small ranks work; when it is low (e.g., after pruning), larger ranks are needed.
(iv) Thus, reinitializing the slice has little effect on final performance, whereas reinitializing or degrading the <em id="A4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">backbone</em> has a large effect—explaining why randomly initialized adapter-style PEFT modules work well when the backbone is strong.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary D.2</span></span><span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Effect of degrading the backbone)<span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the pruning fraction applied to the <em id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">frozen</em> backbone (slice remains trainable).
Let <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}(p)</annotation></semantics></math> be the singular values of <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> after pruning, and define the task-energy ratio</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(p)\;:=\;\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{k_{\mathrm{task}}}\sigma_{j}(p)^{2}}{\sum_{j}\sigma_{j}(p)^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(p)\;:=\;\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{k_{\mathrm{task}}}\sigma_{j}(p)^{2}}{\sum_{j}\sigma_{j}(p)^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a slice mask <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, define the overlap</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{p}^{(r)}(M)\;:=\;\sigma_{\min}\!\big(U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}(p)^{\top}J_{M}^{\phi}(x)\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{p}^{(r)}(M)\;:=\;\sigma_{\min}\!\big(U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}(p)^{\top}J_{M}^{\phi}(x)\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}(p)</annotation></semantics></math> spans the top task subspace of the pruned backbone at layer <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume the alignment and smoothness conditions of Lemma <a href="#A4.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma D.1 (Backbone energy &amp; alignment condition for local winners). ‣ Appendix D Backbone Dependence ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> with constants
<math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c,\rho&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c,\rho&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}=\lambda_{\max}(H_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}=\lambda_{\max}(H_{M})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph">(i) Vanishing guarantee.</em><span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> If <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(p)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(p)=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{p}^{(1)}(M)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{p}^{(1)}(M)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then the lower bound in
Lemma <a href="#A4.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma D.1 (Backbone energy &amp; alignment condition for local winners). ‣ Appendix D Backbone Dependence ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> becomes vacuous:</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big\|\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})\big\|\;\not\geq\;(1-c\rho)\,\gamma_{p}^{(1)}(M)\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}(p)\|\;=\;0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≱</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn> 0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big\|\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})\big\|\;\not\geq\;(1-c\rho)\,\gamma_{p}^{(1)}(M)\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}(p)\|\;=\;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">so there is <em id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p2.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">no guaranteed</em> descent for rank-1 slices. (The gradient may still be nonzero due to the orthogonal component, but the lemma no longer certifies progress.)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph">(ii) Diminishing improvement.</em><span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> If <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(p)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(p)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{p}^{(r)}(M)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{p}^{(r)}(M)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, Lemma <a href="#A4.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma D.1 (Backbone energy &amp; alignment condition for local winners). ‣ Appendix D Backbone Dependence ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> yields</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\min}(p,r)\;\geq\;\frac{(1-c\rho)^{2}}{2L_{p}}\,\big(\gamma_{p}^{(r)}(M)\big)^{2}\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}(p)\|^{2}\;\;\propto\;\;E(p)\,\big(\gamma_{p}^{(r)}(M)\big)^{2}/L_{p}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.838em">∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\min}(p,r)\;\geq\;\frac{(1-c\rho)^{2}}{2L_{p}}\,\big(\gamma_{p}^{(r)}(M)\big)^{2}\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}(p)\|^{2}\;\;\propto\;\;E(p)\,\big(\gamma_{p}^{(r)}(M)\big)^{2}/L_{p}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hence, as pruning increases (<math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>), both <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(p)</annotation></semantics></math> and typically <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{p}^{(r)}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{p}^{(r)}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> decrease, shrinking the certified gain <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\min}(p,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\min}(p,r)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph">(iii) Minimal rank under pruning.</em><span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Define the minimal rank</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\star}(p,\tau)\;:=\;\min\Big\{r:\;\sum_{j=1}^{r}\lambda_{j}(p)\Big/\sum_{j}\lambda_{j}(p)\;\geq\;\tau\Big\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.391em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.055em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}(p,\tau)\;:=\;\min\Big\{r:\;\sum_{j=1}^{r}\lambda_{j}(p)\Big/\sum_{j}\lambda_{j}(p)\;\geq\;\tau\Big\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\lambda_{j}(p)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\lambda_{j}(p)\}</annotation></semantics></math> the PCA eigenvalues of the pruned representation (Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 2.5 (PCA Decomposition of the Representation/Linearized NTK Kernel). ‣ 2 Winner Slices in Pretrained Networks ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>).
Under spectral balance, there exists <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq r^{\star}(p,\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq r^{\star}(p,\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{p}^{(r)}(M)\geq\gamma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{p}^{(r)}(M)\geq\gamma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for admissible slices <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\star}(p,\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}(p,\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> is nondecreasing in <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and gives a sufficient rank to recover a positive, <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-robust guarantee:</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\min}(p,r^{\star})\;\geq\;\frac{(1-c\rho)^{2}\gamma_{0}^{2}}{2L_{p}}\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}(p)\|^{2}\;&gt;\;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn> 0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\min}(p,r^{\star})\;\geq\;\frac{(1-c\rho)^{2}\gamma_{0}^{2}}{2L_{p}}\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}(p)\|^{2}\;&gt;\;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Proof sketch.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Apply Lemma <a href="#A4.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma D.1 (Backbone energy &amp; alignment condition for local winners). ‣ Appendix D Backbone Dependence ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> with <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}(p)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{M}^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{M}^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> evaluated on the pruned backbone.
If <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(p)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(p)=0</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}(p)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}(p)=0</annotation></semantics></math>; if <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{p}^{(1)}(M)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{p}^{(1)}(M)=0</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}(p)^{\top}J_{M}^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}(p)^{\top}J_{M}^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is rank-deficient for rank-1 slices.
Either case collapses the lower bound. When both are positive, the decrease bound scales like
<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(p)\cdot(\gamma_{p}^{(r)}(M))^{2}/L_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(p)\cdot(\gamma_{p}^{(r)}(M))^{2}/L_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 2.5 (PCA Decomposition of the Representation/Linearized NTK Kernel). ‣ 2 Winner Slices in Pretrained Networks ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(p)</annotation></semantics></math> is the PCA CEV of the pruned features; pruning reduces it, and the spectral-balance argument implies that the minimal rank needed to obtain overlap bounded away from zero grows with <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding the monotonicity of <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\star}(p,\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}(p,\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary D.3</span></span><span id="A4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Adapters as local winners)<span id="A4.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a pretrained backbone and insert an adapter with parameters <math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> at layer <math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>
(e.g., parallel linear <math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=W\phi+BA\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=W\phi+BA\phi</annotation></semantics></math> or residual bottleneck <math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{A}(\phi)=\phi+B\,\sigma(A\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{A}(\phi)=\phi+B\,\sigma(A\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>).
Assume we operate in the linearized regime around <math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\theta_{0},A_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\theta_{0},A_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, so</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta_{0},A}(x)\;\approx\;f_{\theta_{0}}(x)+J_{A}(x)\,\mathrm{vec}(A)\,,\qquad J_{A}(x):=\Big[\frac{\partial f}{\partial A}\Big]_{\theta_{0},A_{0}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>vec</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta_{0},A}(x)\;\approx\;f_{\theta_{0}}(x)+J_{A}(x)\,\mathrm{vec}(A)\,,\qquad J_{A}(x):=\Big[\frac{\partial f}{\partial A}\Big]_{\theta_{0},A_{0}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}</annotation></semantics></math> span the task subspace at layer <math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, and define the feature–space adapter Jacobian
<math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{A}^{\phi}(x):=\big[\tfrac{\partial\phi_{\ell}(x)}{\partial A}\big]_{\theta_{0},A_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{A}^{\phi}(x):=\big[\tfrac{\partial\phi_{\ell}(x)}{\partial A}\big]_{\theta_{0},A_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If the backbone has positive task energy <math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> (nontrivial CEV; Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 2.5 (PCA Decomposition of the Representation/Linearized NTK Kernel). ‣ 2 Winner Slices in Pretrained Networks ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>),
satisfies spectral balance, and the adapter has nontrivial overlap</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{A}\;:=\;\sigma_{\min}\!\big(U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}^{\top}J_{A}^{\phi}(x)\big)\;&gt;\;0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn> 0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{A}\;:=\;\sigma_{\min}\!\big(U_{k_{\mathrm{task}}}^{\top}J_{A}^{\phi}(x)\big)\;&gt;\;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then under the alignment (<math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>) and smoothness (<math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>) conditions of Lemma <a href="#A4.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma D.1 (Backbone energy &amp; alignment condition for local winners). ‣ Appendix D Backbone Dependence ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>,
the restricted gradient on the adapter obeys</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big\|\nabla_{A}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})\big\|\;\geq\;(1-c\rho)\,\gamma_{A}\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|\;&gt;\;0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn> 0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big\|\nabla_{A}\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0})\big\|\;\geq\;(1-c\rho)\,\gamma_{A}\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|\;&gt;\;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the one–step <math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth decrease bound holds:</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\!\Big(\theta_{0},A-\tfrac{1}{L}\nabla_{A}\mathcal{L}\Big)\;\leq\;\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0},A)\;-\;\tfrac{(1-c\rho)^{2}}{2L}\,\gamma_{A}^{2}\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.520em;" width="0.520em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\!\Big(\theta_{0},A-\tfrac{1}{L}\nabla_{A}\mathcal{L}\Big)\;\leq\;\mathcal{L}(\theta_{0},A)\;-\;\tfrac{(1-c\rho)^{2}}{2L}\,\gamma_{A}^{2}\,\|g_{\phi,\mathrm{task}}\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hence small adapters act as <em id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">local winners</em> provided the pretrained backbone supplies sufficient task energy and the adapter’s Jacobian overlaps the task subspace.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Ablation Study</h2>

<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Rank vs Winner</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a) analyzes the effect of slice rank on downstream performance.
As the rank increases, accuracy initially improves because larger slices capture more task-relevant directions within the representation subspace.
However, beyond a certain point, adding additional parameters yields diminishing returns: accuracy plateaus or slightly declines due to redundancy and potential overfitting.
This pattern highlights that only a small fraction of the full parameter space is necessary to capture the intrinsic task dimension <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\mathrm{task}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\mathrm{task}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Importantly, when the slice rank equals the full layer dimension, slice training reduces to standard full fine-tuning.
The ablation therefore illustrates a smooth trade-off between efficiency and accuracy: small ranks already achieve strong performance (supporting the <em id="A5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local winner</em> property), while larger ranks approximate full fine-tuning but at greater computational cost.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F11" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.08513v1/switch.png" id="A5.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:545/135;" width="545" height="135" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11: </span>
Accuracy curves for <span id="A5.F11.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SST-2</span>, <span id="A5.F11.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MRPC</span>, <span id="A5.F11.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">QNLI</span>, and <span id="A5.F11.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">STS-B</span> under different
slice update intervals. Each curve corresponds to a policy where the active slice is changed
every <math id="A5.F11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> training steps (e.g., “Change every 50 steps”). The results show that varying the
slice update interval leads to similar performance trends across tasks, indicating that the
spectral balance property allows slices to adapt effectively regardless of update frequency.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Slice Update Interval</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A5.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ E.1 Rank vs Winner ‣ Appendix E Ablation Study ‣ SliceFine: The Universal Winning-Slice Hypothesis for Pretrained Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> analyzes the effect of varying the interval <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> at which the active slice is switched during training.
We consider a spectrum of update frequencies, ranging from very frequent switching (every <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> steps) to very infrequent switching (every <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2000</annotation></semantics></math> steps), across four representative GLUE tasks: SST-2, MRPC, QNLI, and STS-B.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We observe three consistent patterns.
First, when slices are switched too frequently (e.g., every <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> steps), the model exhibits lower accuracy across all datasets.
This degradation occurs because each slice has insufficient time to adapt its parameters before being replaced.
The optimization dynamics become unstable: gradients partially adapt one slice, then abruptly shift to another, preventing any slice from accumulating task-relevant signal.
As a result, rapid switching reduces the effective learning capacity of the model.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, when slices are switched too late (e.g., at 2000 steps), the performance is comparable to other schedules but does not always achieve the best accuracy.
Here, a single slice has ample time to adapt, but since all updates are concentrated on one subspace for a long period, the optimization may overfit or fail to leverage complementary directions from other slices.
This leads to slightly reduced generalization compared to moderate switching intervals.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Third, we find that intermediate switching intervals yield the strongest results, with the optimal value depending on the dataset.
For instance, SST-2 achieves its peak accuracy when switching every <math id="A5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1500</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1500</annotation></semantics></math> steps (rank-1 slice), MRPC benefits most from switching every <math id="A5.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>500</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">500</annotation></semantics></math> steps, STS-B reaches its best accuracy at <math id="A5.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> steps, while QNLI shows stable performance for intervals between <math id="A5.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>500</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">500</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2000</annotation></semantics></math>.
These variations reflect differences in task complexity and the effective task subspace dimension <math id="A5.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\mathrm{task}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\mathrm{task}}</annotation></semantics></math>: tasks with simpler label structures require less adaptation time per slice, while more complex tasks benefit from longer adaptation before switching.</p>
</div>
